,20,60,313,21621,317,1502,20912,35,73315,13030,58,15,1125,3944,1215,2383,20912,34,73315,13030,58,16,1125,45,1215,2383,20912,33,73315,13030,58,17,1125,69214,1215,2383,20912,32,73315,13030,58,18,1125,16844,317,1502,20912,37,73315,13030,58,19,1125,50,1215,2383,20912,36,73315,13030,58,20,1125,925,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nAll of the triangles in the figure and the central hexagon are equilateral. Given that $\\overline{AC}$ is 3 units long, how many square units, expressed in simplest radical form, are in the area of the entire star? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; import graph; size(150); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\npair[] vertices;\nfor(int i = 0; i < 6; ++i){\n\nvertices[i] = dir(30 + 60*i);\n}\ndraw(vertices[0]--vertices[2]--vertices[4]--cycle);\ndraw(vertices[1]--vertices[3]--vertices[5]--cycle);\nlabel(\"$D$\",vertices[0],NE); label(\"$C$\",vertices[1],N); label(\"$B$\",vertices[2],NW); label(\"$A$\",vertices[3],SW);\nlabel(\"$F$\",vertices[4],S); label(\"$E$\",vertices[5],SE);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"fb98ec97ffbc1747ab50d81f4454b2a4067298d8aa503ecf44a5971975db53f0"},"seconds":39.222705125808716,"seed":1093549431782787553,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the area of the star, we first need to analyze the structure and dimensions of the star.\n\n1. **Identify the side length of the equilateral triangles and the central hexagon:**\n\n   Given that \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is 3 units long, we note that \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is a side of one of the outer equilateral triangles. This means each side of the outer equilateral triangles is 3 units.\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one equilateral triangle:**\n\n   The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{s^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4}\n   \\]\n   For \\(s = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{3^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the area of the central hexagon:**\n\n   The central hexagon is composed of 6 smaller equilateral triangles, each with side length 1.5 units (since the hexagon is formed by connecting the midpoints of the sides of the outer triangles).\n\n   Using the area formula for an equilateral triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one small triangle} = \\frac{(1.5)^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{2.25 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{16}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the area of the hexagon is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of hexagon} = 6 \\times \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{16} = \\frac{54\\sqrt{3}}{16} = \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total area of the star:**\n\n   The star consists of 6 outer equilateral triangles and the central hexagon. The area of the 6 outer triangles is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{54\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Adding the area of the central hexagon:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area} = \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8} = \\frac{108\\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8} = \\frac{135\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of the entire star is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{135\\sqrt{3}}{8}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{135\\sqrt{3}}{8}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
